    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/9170": {
      "title": "Landscaping Aide",
      "description": "Category: Facilities Services JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States The Grounds Department at the University of Tennessee at Martin is seeking qualified candidates for the position of Landscaping Aide. This is a regular, full-time position with benefits. The Grounds Department at the University of Tennessee at Martin is seeking qualified candidates for the position of Landscaping Aide. This is a regular, full-time position with benefits. Minimum Qualifications: * Typically requires a high school diploma and six months of relevant experience, or the equivalent combination of education, training and experience. * Must be able to follow written and oral instructions. * Ability to lift up to 75 pounds is required. Must be able to work during severe winter weather (snow and ice) even if campus classes are dismissed and offices are closed. * Experience with lawn equipment and power tools. Application Process: Qualified applicants must apply online Application Deadline: Applicant review will begin immediately and continue until a candidate has been selected Minimum hiring rate $14.25 per hour (Market Range 03)-Salary commensurate with education and experience * Responsible for maintaining and enhancing the safety, appearance, and functionality of the campus grounds. Also, the moving of university furniture and furnishings, and performing set-ups and strikes of tables, chairs, and other furnishings and equipment is required. * Installation and maintenance of ornamental flower beds, shrubs, trees, turf, typical greenhouse work, and hardscape as directed by Grounds Supervisors. This includes, but is not limited to, the cultivation, fertilization, seeding, and mowing of all turf areas. Prune shrubs and trees to specifications. * Participates in the utilization and maintenance of specialized landscape and grounds maintenance equipment. This equipment includes, but is not limited to stump grinder, commercial lawn mowers, tractors, loaders, tillers, weed-eaters, gator type vehicles, sweepers, aerators, vacuum machines, blowers, sprayers, etc. * Participates in the application of chemicals and fertilizers including the use of herbicides, fungicides, insecticides, and growth retardants as directed by Grounds Supervisors. Stores, maintains, mixes, and applies agricultural and commercial chemicals in conformity with state and federal regulations. * Performs and assigns various cleaning and housekeeping tasks as directed by Grounds Supervisor. Makes minor repairs to structures, facilities, and earthworks. Performs litter and debris removal from projects, and on routine basis. Participates in litter control throughout campus. * Cleans, patches, resurfaces, and maintains streets, walks, parking areas, etc. This includes the removal of snow, litter, leaves, and other debris. Apply salt and maintain safety standards on walkways during inclement weather. Erects signs and installs flags as directed by Grounds Supervisor."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/7343": {
      "title": "Associate Division Planner - IPS",
      "description": "Category: Public Service JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Jackson, TN, United States In this new position, the Associate Division Planner is responsible for performing complex and highly responsible professional planning activities for the University of Tennessee’s Institute of Public Service, Center for Local Planning. Work will occur in cooperation with local partners in a specific Grand Division of the State. The position carries out technical assignments including project management, research, data analysis, oral presentations, and development of policy and plan recommendations. The role requires professionalism, tact, and diplomacy in working with a wide range of stakeholders. Reports to the Senior Division Planner. In this new position, the Associate Division Planner is responsible for performing complex and highly responsible professional planning activities for the University of Tennessee’s Institute of Public Service, Center for Local Planning. Work will occur in cooperation with local partners in a specific Grand Division of the State. The position carries out technical assignments including project management, research, data analysis, oral presentations, and development of policy and plan recommendations. The role requires professionalism, tact, and diplomacy in working with a wide range of stakeholders. Reports to the Senior Division Planner. This position will be located in West Tennessee, and may work from the Jackson, Memphis, or Martin IPS office. The position will be hybrid in accordance with the University of Tennessee’s Hybrid and Remote Work Policy. Required Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree required in Planning, Public Administration, Public Policy, Geography, or related field. Experience: Minimum four (4) years of professional planning experience at the City, County, Regional, State or consultant level. Applicants must possess significant practical experience with the legal, political, and administrative aspects of Land Use Planning and Zoning. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Knowledge of principles, best practices, and techniques of rural, urban, and regional planning. Knowledge of laws, regulations, and policies governing land use planning and development in the State of Tennessee. Skill in project management, data analysis, and preparation of technical reports. Skill in effective oral and written communication tailored to diverse audiences. Skilled in planning for growth, redevelopment, and infrastructure investment Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with local officials, staff, and stakeholders. Ability to analyze problems, evaluate alternatives, and recommend sound solutions. Ability to use GIS, mapping, and data visualization tools to support decision making. Knowledge of personal computer applications and digital literacy Ability to meet deadlines and keep commitments to various stakeholders. Ability to travel and present outside normal business hours. Preferred Qualifications Education: Master’s Degree Planning, Public Administration, Public Policy, Geography, or related field is preferred. Experience: Membership in good standing with the American Institute of Certified Planners (AICP) strongly preferred. Knowledge, Skills, Abilities: Demonstrated practical knowledge of the legal, political, and administrative aspects of Land Use Planning and Zoning. Knowledge of state statutes, County/municipal governance, infrastructure, and intergovernmental frameworks in Tennessee. Work Location: The position will be located in West Tennessee, and may work from the Jackson, Memphis, or Martin IPS office. The Institute for Public Service (IPS) supports a formal Alternative Work Arrangement program currently in place. More information can be provided if candidate progresses through the recruitment process. Benefits: Find more information on UT Benefits here Application Instructions: To express interest, please submit an application with the noted below attachments. To be assured of full consideration, completed applications with all requested materials should be submitted. Resume Cover Letter List of 3 Professional References About The College/Department/Division The UT Institute for Public Service provides outreach and assistance to all of Tennessee. Through our agencies and initiatives, we offer technical assistance, training, and consulting on a wide variety of services, including government operations, language services, law enforcement excellence, leadership training, manufacturing excellence, opioid abatement and much more. Technical Assistance Provides technical assistance and impartial advice to local governments on matters related to planning, growth, and development; Attends and presents at meetings of local elected bodies, Planning Commissions, Boards of Zoning Appeals, and other groups; Prepares policies, plans, and educational materials; Coordinates multiple projects simultaneously across jurisdictions; Serves as subject matter expert for a wide variety of stakeholders; Prepares reports, presentations, maps, and graphics for local officials; presents complex information in a clear manner appropriate to the audience at hand. Relationships: Cultivates and builds upon relationships with stakeholders, including local Development Districts, local elected and appointed officials, and their professional staff. Maintains positive working relationships with various agencies of state government and staff at sister IPS agencies. Builds reputation as a reliable and impartial source of expertise and information; demonstrates competence, thoughtfulness, and restraint in all professional interactions. Keeps supervisor informed of local concerns and opportunities; Management & Administration Assists in grant writing and administration; Ensures that all forms established and required by the Agency, Institute, or University are completely and correctly completed, and that all applicable policies are followed; Other Attends professional development meetings, conferences, seminars, training classes, and administrative committees and task forces as assigned (may be internal to IPS or the University) Travel as required"
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/7954": {
      "title": "Clinical Case Manager",
      "description": "Category: Medical JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States Under the direction of the Student Health and Counseling Services Assistant Director, this position fosters students' academic and personal success by providing counseling triage, supporting clinical staff, identifying and responding to students in crisis, and making appropriate referrals. This position facilitates communication within SHCS, across the institution, and between the institution and health care providers. This position works closely with the Wellness Educator to develop and implement health promotion and prevention programs that address campus wellness trends and priorities and to reduce personal, campus, and community wellness risk factors. Under the direction of the Student Health and Counseling Services Assistant Director, this position fosters students' academic and personal success by providing counseling triage, supporting clinical staff, identifying and responding to students in crisis, and making appropriate referrals. This position facilitates communication within SHCS, across the institution, and between the institution and health care providers. This position works closely with the Wellness Educator to develop and implement health promotion and prevention programs that address campus wellness trends and priorities and to reduce personal, campus, and community wellness risk factors. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree and two years of relevant experience (may include internships). Experience assessing and coordinating care for individuals experiencing a mental health crisis. Demonstrated knowledge of the developmental counseling model and crisis response leadership. Demonstrated experience working with a multidisciplinary team and collaborating with other professionals (for example, in this context, Student Affairs, Residential Life, health care providers) to support students. Ability to build and maintain collaborative partnerships with campus and community stakeholders to support student wellness and engagement. Demonstrated skill in designing, implementing, and evaluating outreach and prevention programs using evidence-informed practices. Electronic Medical Records and basic computer knowledge and skills, including timely and professional documentation. Demonstrated public speaking; group facilitation; writing and communication skills. Knowledgeable in health and wellness topics relevant to traditional and non-traditional college students. Ability to work a flexible schedule to include weekend and after hours. Knowledgeable regarding codes and laws relevant to services. Preferred Qualifications Two years of experience in a mental health wellness focused agency. Knowledge and relationships with local health care providers. Prior experience in a university setting. Experience developing and implementing wellness, prevention, or outreach programming for young adults or campus populations. Demonstrated knowledge of the mental health concerns of university students, student development theory, and university or college counseling best practices on a residential college campus. Application Process: Applicants must apply online and submit a cover letter, resume and three professional references Application Deadline: Applicant review will begin immediately and continue until a candidate has been identified Minimum hiring rate $50,185.38 (Market Range 10) - Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Clinical Services: Provide counseling triage and assess students for mental, emotional, or substance abuse concerns. Conduct and interpret mental health assessments to identify appropriate sources of care. Support students in navigating campus and community resources and follow up with students who miss scheduled appointments. Document client encounters and treatment interventions in the electronic health record. Collaborate with inter-professional staff to ensure continuity of care, maintain cooperative relationships among clinical staff, and contribute to the quality and effectiveness of SHCS services. Crisis Intervention: Serve as coordinator of the Crisis Response Team and as a member of the CARE Team. Provide crisis intervention for students experiencing mental health or emotional distress. Participate in crisis on-call rotation and be responsive and available to students in crisis. Coordinate follow-up with parents, faculty, and administrators to support students in resuming their studies, seeking a leave of absence, or withdrawing when necessary. Communicate and coordinate across SHCS staff to ensure continuity of care in crisis response. Campus and Community Outreach and Engagement: Engage students, faculty, staff, and community stakeholders to promote wellness and create supportive environments for student success. Develop, plan, coordinate, and implement wellness-focused prevention and educational programs using evidence-informed practices. Build and maintain productive relationships with campus and community partners to garner resources and collaborative support. Ensure broad access to programs by considering developmental and demographic profiles of the student population and by using diverse formats and strategies. Disseminate program information and contribute to advancing an institutional and community climate of wellness, inclusion, and social justice"
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/6116": {
      "title": "College Academic Advisor, Engineering and Natural Science Department",
      "description": "Category: Student Services JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States Academic advisors serve a caseload of assigned majors to provide guidance toward degree completion. With support from Department Chairs and the Dean, advisors will display a thorough understanding of curriculum procedures and graduation requirements, advocate for students, and work closely with faculty, student success officers, and academic records to improve student outcomes and success at UTM. Advisors are expected to monitor students' progress and provide resources and appropriate academic interventions as needed in support of UT Martin's student retention and success goals. Advisors are responsible for accurate record keeping, proficiency in all softwares and systems used in advising and registration, and participation in retention strategies and campaigns for their assigned caseloads. Academic advisors serve a caseload of assigned majors to provide guidance toward degree completion. With support from Department Chairs and the Dean, advisors will display a thorough understanding of curriculum procedures and graduation requirements, advocate for students, and work closely with faculty, student success officers, and academic records to improve student outcomes and success at UTM. Advisors are expected to monitor students' progress and provide resources and appropriate academic interventions as needed in support of UT Martin's student retention and success goals. Advisors are responsible for accurate record keeping, proficiency in all softwares and systems used in advising and registration, and participation in retention strategies and campaigns for their assigned caseloads. Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field and two years of relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training, and experience. Advanced degree preferred. Working knowledge of the basic principles of academic advising and the capacity to learn all facets of specific degree programs. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to analyze problems and formulate solutions. Ability to provide excellent customer service. Ability to work constructively on a team and to complete work independently. Ability to learn and understand new systems. Application Process: Qualified candidates must apply online and attach a cover letter and resume. Applicant Deadline: Review of applicant will begin immediately and continue until the candidates have been identified. Minimum hiring rate $37,705.02 (Market Range 07) – Salary commensurate with education and experience. Academic Advising -Provide specialized major specific guidance, advocacy, and academic advising to students in an assigned caseload to improve retention and graduation outcomes for students, including course selection, registration, program requirements, and academic policies. -Guide students in developing academic plans aligned with their educational and career goals. -Assist students in understanding and navigating university procedures, such as course withdrawals, course repeat policies, probations, and appeals. -Maintain a thorough working knowledge of all curricular elements of assigned areas and serve on any associated committees or work groups as required. Retention and Student Success -Monitor and analyze student progress and engage in campaigns and research to guide students toward appropriate academic interventions. -Utilize proactive and appreciative advising practices to assist students and identify and mitigate potential barriers to success. -Develop and implement strategies to support retention, persistence, and graduation goals in alignment with college and university initiatives. -Maintain regular communication with faculty and staff to coordinate support for students facing academic or personal challenges. -Identify at-risk students using performance data and proactively conducting outreach to provide guidance, intervention, and referrals to campus resources. Programming and Outreach -Develop and deliver workshops, presentations, and information sessions on academic success, study skills, and degree planning -Support new student orientation, recruitment events, and other programs to promote student engagement and success -Serve as a knowledgeable resource for students, parents, faculty, and staff regarding advising services. Records Management and Reporting -Maintain accurate and confidential student advising records in compliance with FERPA and institutional policies -Prepare reports and summaries for the Dean on advising activities, student progress, and retention data -Ensure student information systems are up-to-date and accurate. Professional Development and Other Duties -Participate in college committees, advising councils, or tasks forces as assigned by the Dean -Engage in ongoing training to stay current with best practices in academic advising and retention strategies -Perform other related duties as assigned to support student success."
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/10579": {
      "title": "Associate Dean - School of Business",
      "description": "Category: Academic JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States The University of Tennessee at Martin (UT Martin) invites nominations and applications for the position of Associate Dean to lead its School of Business. The School of Business, which houses all of the AACSB-accredited business programs and faculty within UT Martin’s broader College of Business, seeks an innovative, collaborative, and visionary leader to partner with the dean and others to advance the College's mission of excellence in business education, research, ethical leadership, and experiential learning. The University of Tennessee at Martin (UT Martin) invites nominations and applications for the position of Associate Dean to lead its School of Business. The School of Business, which houses all of the AACSB-accredited business programs and faculty within UT Martin’s broader College of Business, seeks an innovative, collaborative, and visionary leader to partner with the dean and others to advance the College's mission of excellence in business education, research, ethical leadership, and experiential learning. About the University of Tennessee at Martin UT Martin is a public, regional comprehensive university in the University of Tennessee System that includes the main campus, and six regional centers located throughout West Tennessee. Located in rural Northwest Tennessee, UT Martin is a small-town University for people with big dreams. The main campus is located in the welcoming community of Martin, Tennessee and offers more than 140 academic areas of study in a traditional college setting. UT Martin students experience high-quality academic programs, excellent facilities, and outstanding faculty members whose primary focus is teaching. Fall 2026 enrollment totaled 8,253 students who came to the University from 91 of Tennessee's 95 counties, 43 states, and 20 countries. The University competes in NCAA Division I in both men's and women's athletics and is a member of the Ohio Valley Conference. About the College and School The College of Business at UT Martin supports the regional and global economy through business education, outreach, policy engagement, societal impact, ethical leadership, and community-based partnerships. Through its School of Business, the College offers AACSB-accredited business programs, including undergraduate programs in accounting, economics, finance, information systems, management, and marketing, and an online Master of Business Administration program that prepares students for leadership in an interconnected world. The School enrolls approximately 850 undergraduates and 130 graduate students, and its MBA program is ranked highest among colleges and universities in Tennessee and tied for 59th place nationwide. The new Associate Dean will have the opportunity to work closely with other parts of the College, including the Regional Entrepreneurship & Economic Development (REED) Center and the Tennessee Small Business Development Center (TSBDC), which foster regional economic development by supporting prospective entrepreneurs and owners of existing small businesses with individual consultant services, group training services, and associated technical resources. The new Associate Dean will have a significant opportunity to meaningfully influence the future of the College, including through the development of a new $57.5 million building that is expected to break ground in 2027. The Opportunity The Associate Dean is a full-time, 12-month appointment, providing strategic operational and academic leadership for the School of Business, an AACSB-accredited business school comprising undergraduate and graduate programs. The Associate Dean is responsible for the School’s faculty and staff, academic quality and effective operations, accreditation and continuous improvement activities, and resource and personnel management. Through strategic and mission-centered leadership, the Associate Dean drives performance, enrollment, and student success; facilitates a positive work and learning culture; fosters excellence in teaching, research, and service; and cultivates strong relationships with external partners that will benefit the School and its students. Reporting to the Dean of the College of Business, the Associate Dean works collaboratively with faculty and staff, students, discipline leads, program directors, and senior-level administrators to plan, coordinate, and advance the school’s academic priorities, ensuring they are effectively implemented and sustained over time. The successful candidate will have a minimum of five years of relevant experience in higher education, a record of successful and progressive academic leadership, an earned Ph.D. or other terminal degree in a discipline represented in the School of Business, and a record of teaching and scholarly achievement that is aligned with AACSB faculty standards and suitable for tenure at the full or associate professor level. The salary range for this role is $150,000 to $180,000, commensurate with experience and qualifications. To Apply or Nominate a Potential Candidate To nominate a potential candidate or to inquire further about the position, contact Dr. Tim Todd, Interim Dean of the College of Business, at ttodd12@utm.edu . Applications for the Associate Dean will be considered until the position is filled; however, for full consideration, application materials should be submitted by November 30, 2026 . Applicants should submit: a substantive letter of interest stating how the candidate’s experience and qualifications connect with the desired leadership attributes and themes stated in this profile; a curriculum vitae; and names and contact information for four professional references, including a faculty and staff member who have experienced the candidate’s leadership, a supervisor or administrator who directly observed the candidate’s leadership, and another reference who can speak to the candidate’s leadership and potential in the role of Associate Dean. References will be contacted only if candidates reach the interview stage. General Qualifications The Associate Dean holds faculty rank, with a minimum of five years of relevant experience in higher education, and has a demonstrated record of successful and progressive academic leadership. The Associate Dean possesses an earned Ph.D. or other terminal degree in a discipline represented in the School of Business, and a record of teaching and scholarly achievement that is aligned with AACSB faculty standards. It is expected that the Associate Dean holds tenure in the School of Business. An Associate Dean hired through an external search is expected to apply for Tenure Upon Initial Appointment immediately. The Associate Dean exhibits exceptional organizational, communication, time management, and problem-solving skills, and leads with the following qualities: sound judgment, high integrity, credibility, transparency, fairness, empathy, initiative, responsiveness, and trustworthiness. Through respect, practical solutions, proactivity, and the removal of barriers to effective change, the Associate Dean works well with a variety of stakeholders, builds and leads organizational systems, coordinates complex multi-stakeholder initiatives, and consistently delivers measurable results in an academic environment. Job Duties Teach at least one 3-4 hour course per semester (i.e. Fall/Spring) in the School of Business Administer the School’s diverse array of academic programs on a day-to-day basis (e.g., oversee the effective administration of academic policies, catalog updates, transfer credit evaluations, articulation agreements, student requests, facilities, course scheduling and delivery at multiple sites and through a variety of delivery modes, etc.) Manage faculty and staff (e.g., set behavioral and performance expectations, ensure teaching excellence, oversee service to the School and research/scholarly activities of the faculty, recruit/onboard/mentor faculty and staff, facilitate professional development, drive performance, manage workloads, ensure compliance with policies/standards/administrative directives, handle grievances/disputes and performance interventions, etc.) Evaluate the performance of faculty and staff, including for purposes of annual review, enhanced tenure track review, tenure and/or promotion, periodic post-tenure review, graduate faculty status, etc. Cultivate and manage a positive culture for work and learning that fosters collegiality and collaboration, and advance an environment of high engagement and accountability for faculty and staff, including high engagement with students and external stakeholders Promote effective shared governance structures, including appropriate faculty and staff participation in decision making, and responsiveness to strategic direction from senior leadership Collaborate with faculty and staff, students, discipline leads, the Dean, and other senior administrators on strategic direction, goal setting, and policy development and implementation Foster curricular innovations, development, and improvements, especially related to student success, career readiness, continuous improvement, experiential learning, and alignment with workforce and employer needs Drive enrollment through strategic planning, recruitment in the School’s undergraduate and/or graduate programs, and student success initiatives (e.g., for student retention, progression, on-time graduation, academic success, and career outcomes) Promote student engagement activities (e.g., support of undergraduate research, study abroad, service learning, internships, business-related professional student organizations, case competition teams, consulting clinics, physical and/or digital hubs for peer-to-peer networking, student ambassador programs, pitch competitions, student recognition events, networking events, career development activities and fairs, etc.) Engage in data-centered decision making and improvements that drive success in the School Lead successful continuous improvement and accreditation activities, especially as related to AACSB, such as planning, monitoring, or reporting related to Assurance of Learning or Continuous Improvement Reviews, the translation of accreditation standards into sustainable organizational accountability systems and continuous improvement practices, and the evaluation of faculty qualifications and sufficiency processes Develop and administer School budgets and related donor funds to ensure instructional quality, enrollment growth, student success and career readiness, faculty excellence, effective data and technology infrastructure, and strong community engagement Facilitate external funding in the School through grants, sponsorships, and donor support Develop and steward partnerships with alumni, business and industry leaders, community organizations, and/or donors to support the success of the School and its students Facilitate cross-disciplinary collaborations, especially for innovative curricular development or community engagement Represent the School and/or its programs at internal and external meetings, events, and forums, including recruiting and advising events, career fairs, Advisory Council meetings, and community meetings for professional associations, business groups, and civic clubs to promote School initiatives Produce reports related to the activities of the School If designated, serve as acting Dean when the Dean is not available Fulfill other duties as assigned by the Dean, Provost, or Chancellor"
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/10554": {
      "title": "Child Care Teacher Assistant",
      "description": "Category: Teaching & Learning JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States The Margaret N. Perry Children’s Center at the University of Tennessee at Martin is accepting applications for a full-time Child Care Teacher Assistant. The Margaret N. Perry Children’s Center at the University of Tennessee at Martin is accepting applications for a full-time Child Care Teacher Assistant. This position will assist the Lead Teacher in providing the best quality care and guidance to children in the absence of their parents or guardians. Teaching teams strive to create meaningful experiences for children that extend their cognitive, social, physical, language and socioeconomic development. Teachers provide a respectful learning environment, being sensitive to each child’s individual needs. In this supportive environment, children become secure and capable as they strive to master their world. Minimum Qualifications • High school degree and one year working with children. Bachelor’s degree preferred • Must be physically fit to lift at least 40 pounds • Must be capable of getting up and down from the floor, sitting in the floor, plus standing for extended periods • Possess good interpersonal skills, good written and verbal communication skills • Must be highly motivated and energetic Application Process: Qualified applicants must apply online and attach a resume Application Deadline: Applicant review will begin immediately and continue until a suitable candidate has been selected Minimum hiring rate $14.25 (Market Range 02) - Salary commensurate with education and experience • Help provide for the personal needs of each child enrolled in the classroom • Actively take part in a teaching team to guarantee daily operations of the classroom provides a safe, secure and nurturing environment • Help implement developmentally appropriate activities for children ages birth to 5 years • Supervise children inside the classroom and outside during playtime • Help guide student workers and volunteers to guarantee that daily schedules and routines are followed. • Assist in parent communication about children’s daily routines and progress. • Help provide and maintain a stimulating environment that meets 3-STAR guidelines • Help maintain the classroom and communicate potential problems with the Lead Teacher and/or administrative staff • Help conduct parent-teacher conferences"
    },
    "https://fa-ewlq-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1/job/10527": {
      "title": "Administrative Specialist, College of Education, Health, and Behavioral Sciences",
      "description": "Category: Administrative JobSchedule: Full time PrimaryLocation: Martin, TN, United States The Administrative Specialist provides high-level administrative and operational support to the Dean and the College of Education, Health, and Behavioral Sciences. This position plays an important role in supporting the day-to-day operations of the College by coordinating administrative activities, monitoring budgets and financial transactions, managing college-level data, facilitating communication, and assisting with college events and initiatives. The Administrative Specialist provides high-level administrative and operational support to the Dean and the College of Education, Health, and Behavioral Sciences. This position plays an important role in supporting the day-to-day operations of the College by coordinating administrative activities, monitoring budgets and financial transactions, managing college-level data, facilitating communication, and assisting with college events and initiatives. The position works closely with faculty, staff, students, university offices, and other campus and community partners to ensure information is accurate, communication is effective, and administrative processes are completed efficiently. The successful candidate will be organized, detail-oriented, resourceful, and comfortable managing multiple priorities while maintaining confidentiality and providing responsive service to a wide range of constituents. This is a diverse role that provides the opportunity to contribute directly to the effectiveness of the Dean’s Office and the success of the College through administrative coordination, data and budget management, communication, and event support. Minimum Qualifications * High School diploma with two of more years of office management experience. * Excellent oral and written communication skills, including strong grammar, editing, and proofreading abilities * Strong interpersonal and customer service skills, with the ability to communicate effectively with a diverse range of individuals * Demonstrated ability to work independently, take initiative, organize priorities, manage multiple tasks, and maintain a high level of attention to detail * Ability to collaborate effectively with individuals and departments across campus and with members of the community * Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, including Word, Excel, Outlook, and related applications * Ability to learn and effectively use complex computer systems, including DASH, Banner, scheduling software, and other systems as needed * Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise appropriate judgment when handling sensitive information * Ability to work effectively as part of a team and adapt to changing priorities, procedures, and organizational needs * Commitment to providing responsive, supportive, and student-focused service (Every Student, Every Time!) * Ability to recruit, hire, train, and supervise student workers Preferred Qualifications * Bachelor's degree with two or more years of experience on a university campus. * Proficient in Adobe Suite * Data visualizations Application Process: Qualified applicants must apply online and attach a cover letter and resume. Application Deadline: Applicant review will begin immediately and will continue until a candidate has been identified. Hiring range $18.00 - $22.50 per hour (Market Range 06) - Salary is commensurate with education and experience. Reconciliation of monthly ledge reports, entering invoices, travel, payroll and procurement card verification, allotment of budget to specific EO and RO accounts, and working awareness of the balance of the CEHBS operational budge per policies and procedures, while reviewing all departmental budgets within the college. Ensures fiscal compliances with governing organizational rules and regulations. Purchases equipment and office supplies. Data Management: Maintain an active plan for college-level data including: - Enrollment, student credit hour production, majors - Retention and persistence - Admits and registration Communication: - Monthly updates to OneSpace platform - Monthly updates from the Dean's Office - General faculty/staff communication Administrative support: Daily correspondence, emails, committee agenda, and committee minutes for the Dean and the college. Maintains personnel files on all college faculty and staff. Event Planning: - Fall retreat - Spring Symposium - Student success initiatives"
    }
  },
